  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 126 PM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A moisture-starved clipper low and frontal system \nwill bring below average temperatures for Friday through mid next \nweek. Some fire concerns may develop this weekend ahead of the \nwidespread freezing conditions.\n\nNearly all guidance now has continued to trend drier with the \nmoisture-starved clipper low that is forecast to track to our north \nThursday into Friday. With this northward trend, little if any \nrainfall is forecast across central NC as the bulk of the storm's \nenergy focuses across the Mid-Atlantic and Ohio Valley. With that, \nmost of our area will be under a dry downslope pre and post-frontal. \nThe main cold front pushes through Thursday night, while a \nreinforcing crosses the region Friday night. There could still be a \nchance of a few sprinkles or light rain Friday as a secondary \nshortwave digs across the Carolinas and Virginia, but even then \nmoisture will be lacking.\n\nAfter the frontal passage, highs will go from the upper 60s to low \n70s Thursday to the low to middle 50s Friday through mid next week. \nThis is about 10 degrees below normal, with the chilliest day \nexpected Saturday. Widespread freezing temperatures still appear \nlikely Sunday and Monday morning, when the pressure gradient finally \nrelaxes from gusty NW winds Friday and Saturday and CAA abates. \nDuring this period, lows are favored to be in the upper 20s to low \n30s. Continued below average temperatures and consecutive mornings \nof damaging cold will continue into early next week as the cold \nsurface high remains in place.\n\nWith little if any rain forecast from the system, attention turns to \npossible fire concerns with the ongoing drought. The concerned time \nperiod is Friday and Saturday, when there could be an overlap of \ngusty NW winds of 20-30 mph and RH in the 30-35 percent range. Right \nnow, conditions appear marginal, especially given chilly daytime \ntemperatures and wind gusts slightly below IFD criteria. But this is \nsomething we will monitor as the week progresses.",
